{"qid":"Q3037","names":["Kathmandu"],"title":"Kathmandu","endonym":{"native":"काठमांडौ","lang":"hi"},"capsule":"Kathmandu here represents the Malla kingdom of the Nepal Valley, which took shape in the early thirteenth century as rulers from the Tirhut region fled north after Delhi Sultanate pressure and intermarried with existing Nepali nobility, launching over five centuries of Malla rule. At its height the valley comprised four competing city-states — Kantipur (Kathmandu), Lalitpur (Patan), Bhaktapur, and Kirtipur — whose kings sponsored the Durbar Square complexes, the Nyatapola temple, and a trade currency standard adopted across the trans-Himalayan route. Internal rivalries among the Malla courts proved fatal: in 1768 the Gorkha king Prithvi Narayan Shah exploited Malla disunity, capturing Kathmandu on the night of the Indra Jatra festival, and completed annexation of all four cities by 1769, making Kathmandu the capital of a unified Nepal.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Malla-period political history of the Nepal Valley is well-documented in Nepalese chronicles (vamsavalis) and Tibetan sources; precise valley boundary extents in digital form carry moderate uncertainty as they reflect interpretations of historical administrative divisions rather than surveyed frontiers.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kathmandu","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kathmandu"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Malla kingdom (Nepal)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malla_kingdom_(Nepal)"},{"label":"Britannica: Kathmandu","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kathmandu"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":nu